For purposes of this article:
Commercial tenant means any individual, partnership, corporation, or entity legally occupying a commercial space within the city pursuant to a written or oral lease, license, or rental agreement, and operating an active business at that location.
Commercial property owner means the fee simple owner or authorized representative of the owner of commercial real property located within the corporate limits of the City of College Park.
Financial interest means any interest, whether personal or through a business entity, involving potential or actual financial gain, loss, obligation, or benefit to a member, including but not limited to:
(1) Ownership of property, leasehold interests, or business assets that may be affected by a matter before the commission;
(2) Receipt of income, compensation, commissions, or fees from any person or entity that may be affected by a matter before the commission;
(3) An investment, partnership interest, creditor interest, or debtor relationship with any person or entity that has a matter before the commission;
(4) A financial relationship involving an immediate family member, including a spouse, domestic partner, parent, child, or sibling, that may reasonably be expected to influence the member's impartiality;
(5) Any interest requiring disclosure or recusal under Georgia law, including but not limited to O.C.G.A. §§ 36-30-5 and 45-10-3, and applicable conflict-of-interest provisions of the Georgia Constitution; and
(6) Any interest that a reasonable person would conclude is likely to influence the member's judgment in the performance of their official duties.
(Ord. No. 2025-26, § 1(Exh. A), 12-1-25)

658:
The commission shall meet at least quarterly and may meet more frequently as necessary to fulfill its duties. All meetings shall be public and comply with the Georgia Open Meetings Act, O.C.G.A. § 50-14-1 et seq.
(a) Required officers. The commission shall elect from among its voting members the following officers, who shall serve two (2) year terms and may be re-elected: Chair, vice chair, and secretary. Officers shall be elected at the commission's first meeting of each applicable election year or as soon thereafter as practicable.
(b) Chair. The chair shall preside over all meetings of the commission, establish meeting agendas in coordination with the secretary, ensure orderly conduct of business, and serve as the primary liaison between the commission and city staff. The chair may call special meetings as necessary.
(c) Vice chair. The vice chair shall perform the duties of the chair during the chair's absence or incapacity and shall assist the chair in coordinating commission activities. If the chair position becomes vacant, the vice chair shall serve as acting chair until a new chair is elected.
(d) Secretary. The secretary shall be responsible for the accurate and lawful administration of the commission's records and meeting procedures. The secretary shall be an active officer with substantive responsibilities, and failure to perform such duties may be grounds for removal or re-election at the discretion of the commission. The duties of the secretary shall include, but not be limited to:
(1) Ensuring that all commission meetings are properly noticed, advertised, and conducted in full compliance with the Georgia Open Meetings Act, O.C.G.A. § 50-14-1 et seq.;
(2) Posting and distributing all required meeting notices, agendas, and schedules as required by state law and city policy, including ensuring that such information is made available on the city's website or other designated public platforms;
(3) Preparing, maintaining, and preserving accurate minutes of all commission meetings, ensuring that such minutes contain all information required by state law, and ensuring that approved minutes are made publicly available;
(4) Assisting the city clerk in responding to public records requests related to the commission by maintaining organized, complete, and accessible records and serving as the primary point of contact for commission-related Open Records Act responsibilities, subject to coordination with the city clerk as custodian of city records;
(5) Maintaining agendas, minutes, reports, bylaws, officer rosters, and all other official documents of the commission and transmitting such records to the city clerk for archival retention in accordance with state-records retention laws; and
(6) Working with city staff assigned to support the commission to ensure efficient operations, proper recordkeeping, and timely submission of the commission's annual report and work plan.
(Ord. No. 2025-26, § 1(Exh. A), 12-1-25)
